# EcoSuit MK.I — Vacuum-Gap Electrostatic Actuators
**V-EMS / V-EBM · plate latches, visors, deployables — not gait motors**

Vacuum-gap electrostatic actuators flip the usual space-hardware complaint. Motors overheat because vacuum will not convect. Oils freeze or outgas. Dielectric-elastomer stacks want soft polymers that are not on the materials list. These devices treat the vacuum itself as the dielectric and as the moving volume.

Primary source: Sîrbu, Mazzotta, Fontana et al., Nature Communications 16:11414 (2025). Family name **V-EMS**. Building block **V-EBM** (vacuum electro-bonded membrane).

---

## 1. How they work

Two metallized polymer films sit face to face, bonded only at a rim. In vacuum the pocket between them is empty. An external tension opens that pocket into a wedge: gap ≈ 0 at one edge, large at the other.

Apply kilovolts. Opposite charges on the two electrodes pull the films together. Attraction is strongest where the gap is already smallest, so a **zip front** runs across the cell and the pocket collapses. The cell shortens. Drop the voltage and the preload opens it again.

That kinematics is the same family as electrostatic zippers and HASEL pouches on Earth — except the “fluid” is vacuum, so there is nothing to boil, freeze, leak, or viscously resist the collapse.

Why vacuum is the point, not a nuisance:
- Very high dielectric strength once past the Paschen minimum
- Essentially zero conductivity
- Zero viscosity, so the gap can slam shut
- No liquid dielectric to qualify, contain, or outgas

Electrostatic pressure scales as:

    p = 1/2 · ε0 · εr · E²

In the zipped zone, E is set by the polymer thickness (tens of microns), not by the open gap. That is how a floppy film pair produces newtons instead of millinewtons.

