## 2. What has actually been built

| Metric | Demonstrated |
|---|---|
| Mass (optimized unit) | 0.7 g (~1/9 of an equivalent liquid-gap EBM) |
| Blocked force | >4 N; ~4.25 N at 6 kV, ~3 N at 5 kV, ~2.2 N at 4 kV |
| Stroke | ~1 mm on the light unit; up to 3.6 mm on a 3-unit stack at 1 N preload |
| Contraction | ~40% (light unit), approaching ~50% on the stack at low preload |
| Bandwidth | >100 Hz |
| Peak specific power | 1.4 kW/kg |
| Drive | 4–6 kV; current is tiny |
| Environment | 5×10⁻⁵ to 10⁻⁶ mbar in the paper; still works down to ~10⁻² mbar; dies above that |
| Atmosphere | Does not work in air |
| Materials | Polyimide films (~25 µm), space-grade polymers, thin anti-arc coating |
| Other dielectric candidates | PTFE, Mylar, nylon, Parylene |
| Cycle life (supporting work) | >5×10⁵ cycles |
| Environment coupons | Dielectric strength held through ~145 °C and high gamma dose |
| Demo article | Small vacuum gripper, not a walking robot |

A three-unit stack is mechanically in series (stroke adds) and electrically in parallel (one HV bus).

Unit stack-up:
- Rigid ring frame
- Two PI films (~25 µm)
- ~5 µm annular adhesive
- Compliant electrodes
- Thin insulating overcoat (cuts vacuum-arc rate on fast edges)
- Gas outlet so the pocket equalizes to chamber vacuum

