## 3. Family tree

| Relative | Gap dielectric | Needs vacuum? | Works in air? | Typical issue |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| DEA (dielectric elastomer) | Stretchy rubber | No | Yes | Breakdown; few space-grade elastomers |
| HASEL / liquid zipper | Oil / dielectric fluid | No | Yes | Fluid fails or freezes in vacuum |
| Air-gap zipper | Air | No | Yes, with care | Paschen / corona at kV |
| **V-EBM / V-EMS** | **Vacuum** | **Yes** | **No** | Dead in atmosphere and in dirty vacuum |
| Comb / inchworm electrostatics | Air or vacuum | Sometimes | Sometimes | Tiny force; lithography-scale |

V-EBMs are not DEAs with the rubber deleted. DEAs need the elastomer to strain. V-EBMs need the gap to disappear. Different physics, different materials list, opposite atmosphere requirement.

---

## 4. Constraints that matter more than W/kg

### Paschen, not vacuum, is the enemy
High vacuum is safe. Cabin air is safe for these parts only because they will not run. The dangerous band is rough / medium vacuum — airlock pump-down, a leaking PLSS bay, ascent. Above ~10⁻² mbar the residual gas ionizes and the cell stops or arcs.

Design rule: inhibit the HV supply unless a gauge says the environment is hard enough.

### They do not work inside the pressure garment
The EcoSuit interior is ~3–8 psi of gas. That is the wrong dielectric. Sealing a vacuum pouch and calling it the same actuator reinvented a HASEL and lost the vacuum advantage. V-EBMs belong on the vacuum side of the ceramic.

### Force is real. Stroke is short. Gait torque is not there yet
4 N at 1 mm is 4 mJ per stroke. A knee wanting 30–40 N·m through 1 rad wants tens of joules and a long lever. Stacking cells and adding linkage builds a linear muscle tape, not a hip SEA. Treat them as mechanisms, not walk motors.

### Kilovolts next to a human
Current is low, so stored energy can be small, but 6 kV on the outside of a suit still wants:
- potting
- creepage distance
- bleed-down resistors
- hard inhibit tied to hatch / airlock state

Polarity reversal is already used to fight interfacial charge-up. That drive waveform lives in the PLSS electronics, not as an afterthought.

### Charge dynamics
Multilayer electrostatic stacks accumulate interface charge and force sags under DC. Liquid-gap cousins need dielectric matching or AC inversion. Vacuum-gap parts still need a drive strategy (the paper used alternating polarity). Long holds (a latch left closed for six hours) are a materials-and-drive problem, not a solved one.

### Dust
A zip front is two films kissing. Lunar or Martian fines in that interface are a short, a puncture, or a stuck cell. External EcoSuit use means covers, labyrinths, or cells that live under a plate until they fire.

### Not backdrivable in the SEA sense
Off: two floppy films plus whatever spring the frame gives you.
On: they clamp.
That is a latch personality, not an impedance-control personality.

