## 7. How to make them (factory notes)

1. Space-grade PI (or PTFE / Mylar) film, ~25 µm, metallize one face.
2. Pattern electrodes. Leave a creepage margin at the rim.
3. Bond two films on an annular adhesive (~5 µm) to a rigid ring frame. Leave a gas outlet so the pocket can equalize to chamber vacuum.
4. Apply the thin insulating overcoat that cut arcing in the paper.
5. Stack: mechanical series for stroke, electrical parallel for one HV line.
6. Pot the HV feedthrough. Add a bleed resistor and a pressure-switch inhibit.
7. Qualify as a mechanism, not as a motor:
   - thermal-vac
   - TVAC arc / Paschen sweep
   - ~145 °C class
   - radiation coupon
   - dust
   - cycle life
   - suit-latch load case

Outgassing and NASA-STD-6001 still apply. The selling point is that the moving dielectric is vacuum, so the materials can be the same Kapton / Teflon / Mylar already in the harness book.

---

## 8. Split with the rest of the suit

| Layer | Hardware | Job |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | Springs, clutches | Cancel suit hysteresis; fail-open |
| 1 | Rotary SEAs at hip / knee | Walk, kneel |
| 2 | Cable-SEAs from the dorsal lattice | Shoulder, elbow, ankle |
| 3 | Tendon / SMA / PAM | Glove |
| **V-EBM** | **Vacuum-gap cells on the exterior** | **Move the plates** |

SEAs and cables move the human.
Vacuum-gap cells move the plates.